<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chalcis</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Chalcis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Euboean city on the Euripus strait that pioneered Greek colonisation in Italy, Sicily and the Chalcidice and spread its alphabet to the Etruscans and Romans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chalcis grew on the narrowest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripus-strait">Euripus</a> channel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Ionian in dialect, it was an early trading power by the 8th century BCE. Its long rivalry with nearby <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a> for the Lelantine plain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelantine-war">Lelantine War</a>, drew in allies from much of Greece.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a>, Chalcidians founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithekoussai">Pithekoussai</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; alone they founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leontini">Leontini</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catana">Catana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhegion">Rhegium</a> and many towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>. Their version of the alphabet passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>. Chalcis was governed by a landowning aristocracy, the hippobotai, or horse-breeders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> defeated Chalcis in 506 BCE and settled colonists on its land. After revolts against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> it became a Macedonian stronghold, one of the 'fetters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>', and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> died there in 322 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sacked it in 146 BCE for joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>'s war.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chalcis.jpg" alt="Chalcis" width="320" height="202"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A7%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%BA%CE%AF%CE%B4%CE%B1_0067.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalcis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chalkis</dd></dl></aside></div>

